From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Jordan <scott@spaceshipcreative.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 05:19:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/25] [Bugfix][GLM5Next] Deterministic, bounds-safe kpool
 indexer top-k

Three changes to the GLM5Next (GLM-5.3) NVIDIA kpool sparse indexer.

(a) -1 initialization. The model and MTP topk_indices_buffer and the
    per-call pool top-k tensors (prefill and decode) were torch.empty, so
    any slot a kernel leaves unwritten (padded rows, padding columns, rows
    shorter than k) held garbage indices. Allocate them with -1.

(b) Pool id range mask. _expand_pools_and_append_tail_kernel only checked
    pid >= 0. A pool id at or past pool_len (= seq_len // kpool, the
    complete pools of the row) would expand to tokens beyond the sequence
    or duplicate the tail, so treat it as invalid (-1).

(c) Deterministic top-k, gated by VLLM_GLM5NEXT_DETERMINISTIC_TOPK
    (default 1). The radix top-k kernels return the right pool set but in a
    launch-dependent order, and keep a launch-dependent subset of pools
    tied at the k-th score. Pools expand to token indices in list order and
    sparse MLA accumulates in that order, so identical requests past
    index_topk (2048) tokens diverge. New Triton kernel
    canonicalize_pool_topk (nvidia/ops/pool_topk.py) rewrites each row to
    the exact top-k with ties going to the lowest pool id, ids ascending,
    -1 padded:
    - prefill asks top_k_per_row_prefill for TOPK_TIE_SLACK (32) extra
      candidates (capped at 2048), so boundary ties normally resolve from
      the candidates;
    - decode keeps the dispatcher's k, so the backend choice
      (persistent/cooperative/per_row/...) is unchanged;
    - when the k-th score equals the worst candidate's and the row has more
      pools, the kernel rescans the row in id order (all scores above the
      k-th, then the lowest-id ties).
    The shared top-k dispatcher is not modified. Cost: one extra small
    Triton launch per indexer layer and step, plus a row scan for rows
    whose tie may extend past the candidates (every long decode row, since
    decode has no extra candidates).

Provenance:
- (a) and (b) are from MiaAI-Lab glm53-flash_SM121.py
  (bake_indexer_topk_neg1, bake_kpool_pid_clamp; MIT, Copyright (c) 2026
  MiaAI-Lab), applied to v0.31.0's file layout and extended to the model
  and MTP buffers.
- (c) follows the approach Kindling (glm-5.3-flash-gx10) described in
  dev/TOPK-CORRUPTION.md and its deterministic-topk patch (extra
  candidates, lowest-id tie-break, ascending rows). That patch has no
  license, so no code was taken from it; this kernel is an independent
  implementation (int32 sort-based selection, different structure).

Tests: tests/models/glm5next/test_kpool_canonical_topk.py (CUDA-only)
checks the kernel against a reference for many candidate draws with heavy
ties, ragged (prefill) and dense (decode) layouts, slack 0/1/32, a strided
destination, and end to end after top_k_per_row_prefill.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
